import json
from pathlib import Path
p=Path(__file__).parent;r=json.loads((p/'reviews.json').read_text())
N='https://www.azoo.hr/app/uploads/uvezeno/datoteke/TESTOVI/mat-drzavno.zip'
H='https://dev.skoljka.org/media/documents/cc89d67e-aab5-4076-a2f3-b7947e3481de/hmo_2019_rj.pdf'
def add(slug,section,n,v,topic,why,st,sol,src,notes=''):
 comp='Croatian National Mathematical Competition' if 'National' in section else 'Croatian Mathematical Olympiad'
 (p/(slug+'.json')).write_text(json.dumps({'country':'Croatia','competition':comp,'year':2019,'section':section,'problem_number':str(n),'problem_markdown':st,'solutions_markdown':[]},ensure_ascii=False,indent=2))
 r[slug]={'difficulty':v,'topic':topic,'tags':[],'reason':why,'sourceUrl':src,'solutionUrl':src,'solutionKind':'original-exposition','solution':sol,'sourceName':'Croatian Mathematical Society — official 2019 solutions'+(' mirrored by Školjka' if src.startswith(H) else ' from AZOO'),'license':'Original competition rights retained; English mathematical paraphrase and original exposition.','notes':notes,'number':str(n)}
for year,n,v,topic,why,st in [
 (1,3,3,'Algebra','A shared lower bound on the three denominators reduces the sum to one rational function of the sum of squares.',r'Positive $a,b,c$ satisfy $a+b+c=1$. Prove $\sum_{\rm cyc}(1+9a^2)/(1+2a+2b^2+2c^2)<4$.'),
 (2,3,3.5,'Geometry','The side relation makes the altitude four inradii; the tangent is a midline and the requested intersection is a centroid.',r'In $ABC$, $AB+AC=3BC$. A tangent to the incircle parallel to $BC$ meets $AB,AC$ at $K,L$, and $T\in AC$ satisfies $AT=AC/4$. Determine the ratio in which $BT$ divides $KL$.'),
 (3,4,3,'Algebra','Bound the other two squares by half their sum squared; each individual fraction is at most one.',r'For positive $a,b,c$ with $a+b+c=3$, prove $\sum_{\rm cyc}(a^2+6)/(2a^2+2b^2+2c^2+2a-1)\le3$.'),
 (4,3,3.5,'Algebra','Swapping indices makes the constant zero, and consecutive indices give arithmetic running means; recover the sequence by subtraction.',r'Let $M_n=(a_1+\cdots+a_n)/n$. Suppose $(i-j)M_k+(j-k)M_i+(k-i)M_j$ has a constant value for all distinct positive $i,j,k$. Prove that $(a_n)$ is arithmetic.')]:
 add(f'hrv_2019_nat{year}_{n}',f'National Competition — Category A, Year {year}',n,v,topic,why,st,'Below the target difficulty; official solution inspected.',N)
add('hrv_2019_nat3_3','National Competition — Category A, Year 3',3,4.5,'Combinatorics','Count covered grid vertices column by column to sharpen the area bound; a separated rectangular arrangement reaches the bound.',r'Place grid-aligned $3\times1$ rectangles on a $20\times19$ grid. Their boundaries must not touch, even at a vertex. What is the greatest possible number?',r'The maximum is **50**. Each rectangle uses eight grid vertices and contributes an even number of vertices to each vertical grid line. Take the board to have height 20 and width 19. Each of its twenty vertical grid lines contains 21 vertices, so at most twenty can be used. Consequently $8N\le20\cdot20$, giving $N\le50$. To attain this, use horizontal rectangles in rows $1,3,\ldots,19$, and in each such row occupy columns $1$–$3$, $5$–$7$, $9$–$11$, $13$–$15$, and $17$–$19$. The empty rows and columns separate all rectangles.',N)
add('hrv_2019_nat3_5','National Competition — Category A, Year 3',5,5.5,'Geometry','Several orthic cyclic quadrilaterals identify fixed angles; a sine-rule calculation converts both circumcentre conditions into the same angle relation.',r'An acute triangle $ABC$ has $BC<CA<AB$. Let $D,E,F$ be the feet of the altitudes from $A,B,C$. The parallel to $DE$ through $F$ meets line $BC$ at $M$. The internal bisector of $\angle MFE$ meets line $DE$ at $N$. Prove that $F$ is the circumcentre of $DMN$ exactly when $B$ is the circumcentre of $FMN$.',r'Put $\alpha=\angle A<\beta=\angle B<\gamma=\angle C$. The cyclic quadrilaterals $ABDE$, $BCEF$, and $CAFD$, together with $FM\parallel DE$, give $\angle BMF=\alpha$, $\angle MFB=\beta-\alpha$, $\angle MFE=2\beta$, and $\angle BFN=\alpha$. They also show $FM=FD$; write this common length as $R$ and put $BF=b$, $FN=n$. The sine rule in $BMF$ gives $R=b\sin\beta/\sin\alpha$. Since $DN\parallel FM$, resolving $FD$ and $FN$ perpendicular to $FM$ gives $n\sin\beta=R\sin2\alpha$. Hence $n=2b\cos\alpha$, so the cosine rule in $BFN$ yields $BN=b$. Thus $B$ is the required circumcentre exactly when $BM=b$, or $\sin(\beta-\alpha)=\sin\alpha$, equivalently $\beta=2\alpha$. Meanwhile $F$ is the required circumcentre exactly when $n=R$, or $\sin2\alpha=\sin\beta$. This gives either $\beta=2\alpha$ or $\beta=180^\circ-2\alpha$; the latter would imply $\gamma=\alpha$, impossible. Both conditions therefore coincide.',N)
add('hrv_2019_hmo_day1_1','Day 1',1,6,'Combinatorics','After the final collision, nearest-neighbour directions form two blocks. A growing maximum gap fixes those blocks, and a decreasing sum forces finite-time approach to an endpoint.',r'Two fixed directors stand 1000 km apart. Finitely many mathematicians occupy distinct points strictly between them. Every second all mathematicians simultaneously move halfway towards a nearest person; ties are resolved arbitrarily. If two arrive together, the younger leaves. Prove that after finitely many seconds every remaining mathematician is within one metre of a director.',r'Use metres, so the endpoints are $0,L$ with $L=10^6$. There can be only finitely many collisions, since each removes someone. Consider the process after its last collision. Write the remaining positions as $0<x_1<\cdots<x_m<L$. A right-moving person cannot be immediately followed by a left-moving person: they would meet. Thus the directions are a left-moving block $1,\ldots,k$ followed by a right-moving block. The consecutive gaps increase weakly up to the gap between these blocks and decrease weakly afterwards, because each person chose a nearest neighbour. In the next step that separating gap strictly increases, whereas every other gap is an average of adjacent old gaps, or half an endpoint gap. It is therefore the unique largest gap. Since any subsequent direction split must occur at a largest gap, the same two blocks persist. This argument also covers an empty block, when the separating gap is an endpoint gap. The sum $x_1+\cdots+x_k$ decreases by $x_k/2$ each step. If $x_k$ stayed above one, this nonnegative sum would eventually become negative. Hence the whole left block enters the first metre in finite time and stays there. Apply the same argument to distances from $L$ for the right block.',H+'#page=1','The exposition handles arbitrary ties and uses metres consistently; the mirrored draft switches units in its final estimate.')
add('hrv_2019_hmo_imo_1','Final test for IMO team selection',1,5.5,'Algebra','Normalize two values using the candidate family, derive a symmetric product identity, and compare the scalings at two and four.',r'Find all functions $f:\mathbb R_{>0}\to\mathbb R$ such that $$\left(x+\frac1x\right)f(y)=f(xy)+f(y/x)\qquad(x,y>0).$$',r'The solutions are $f(t)=At+B/t$ for arbitrary real $A,B$. These satisfy the equation. Given any solution, choose $A,B$ to match its values at $1,2$, and subtract this candidate. The resulting function $g$ still satisfies the equation and has $g(1)=g(2)=0$. Setting $y=1$ gives $g(1/x)=-g(x)$. Combine the equations for $(x,y)$ and $(y,x)$ to obtain $$2g(xy)=(x+1/x)g(y)+(y+1/y)g(x).$$ Also the original equation at $(2,2)$ gives $g(4)=0$. The displayed identity now yields $g(2y)=\tfrac54g(y)$ and $g(4y)=\tfrac{17}{8}g(y)$. Iterating the first gives $g(4y)=\tfrac{25}{16}g(y)$, so $g(y)=0$ for every $y>0$.',H+'#page=13')
add('hrv_2019_hmo_memo_2','Final test for MEMO team selection',2,5,'Combinatorics','A three-periodic weighting of diagonals makes every allowed tile have weight zero modulo seven, but leaves the board with nonzero weight.',r'Can a $1000\times1000$ square grid be tiled by horizontal or vertical $3\times1$ rectangles and translates of the five-cell shape with row-column coordinates $$\{(0,2),(1,1),(1,2),(2,0),(2,1)\}?$$ The five-cell shape may not be rotated or reflected, and tiles may not overlap.',r'No. Give cell $(i,j)$ weight $2^{i+j}$ modulo 7, numbering rows and columns from zero. A straight three-cell tile has total weight a multiple of $1+2+4=7$. The five-cell tile has relative weight $3\cdot4+2\cdot8=28$, also zero modulo 7; translation multiplies its weight by a power of two. But in a row, the first 999 weights split into triples of sum zero, leaving the last weight. Repeating this down the final column shows that the entire board has the weight of its bottom-right cell, which is nonzero modulo 7. A tiling is therefore impossible.',H+'#page=17','The five-cell shape was checked visually against the original diagram and specified by coordinates to make the question self-contained.')
(p/'reviews.json').write_text(json.dumps(r,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2))
